90,66,92,1124,15136,320,18,1124,10374,13233,90,64,92,481,220,17,1124,10374,13233,90,65,16630,3,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nLet $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ $\\mathbf{c}$ be three vectors such that\n\\[\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b} = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 18 \\end{pmatrix}.\\]Compute $\\mathbf{c} \\times (3 \\mathbf{a} - 2 \\mathbf{b}).$","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"ac5ef49306d7444461100232753035309998368e5c52da3dfc79255650337bae"},"seconds":54.948097467422485,"seed":546279796255667894,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve for $\\mathbf{c} \\times (3 \\mathbf{a} - 2 \\mathbf{b})$, we will use vector properties and the given information.\n\nGiven:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b} = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 18 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nWe need to find $\\mathbf{c} \\times (3 \\mathbf{a} - 2 \\mathbf{b})$.\n\nFirst, use the distributive property of the cross product:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{c} \\times (3 \\mathbf{a} - 2 \\mathbf{b}) = \\mathbf{c} \\times (3 \\mathbf{a}) - \\mathbf{c} \\times (2 \\mathbf{b}).\n\\]\n\nThis can be simplified using the scalar property of the cross product:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{c} \\times (3 \\mathbf{a}) = 3 (\\mathbf{c} \\times \\mathbf{a}) \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\mathbf{c} \\times (2 \\mathbf{b}) = 2 (\\mathbf{c} \\times \\mathbf{b}).\n\\]\n\nNext, we use the property that $\\mathbf{c} \\times \\mathbf{a} = -(\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c})$ and $\\mathbf{c} \\times \\mathbf{b} = -(\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c})$:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{c} \\times \\mathbf{a} = -\\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -4 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathbf{c} \\times \\mathbf{b} = -\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 18 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -1 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -18 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nNow, substitute these into the expressions:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{c} \\times (3 \\mathbf{a}) = 3 \\begin{pmatrix} -4 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ -21 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathbf{c} \\times (2 \\mathbf{b}) = 2 \\begin{pmatrix} -1 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -18 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 14 \\\\ -36 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nFinally, subtract these two results:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{c} \\times (3 \\mathbf{a} - 2 \\mathbf{b}) = \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ -21 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix} - \\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 14 \\\\ -36 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -12 - (-2) \\\\ -21 - 14 \\\\ -6 - (-36) \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -10 \\\\ -35 \\\\ 30 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -10 \\\\ -35 \\\\ 30 \\end{pmatrix}}.\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -10 \\\\ -35 \\\\ 30 \\end{pmatrix}}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
